<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Baroda State</h1><p class="st-meta">1721 CE – 1949 CE (approx.) · capital Vadodara</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Maratha kingdom of the Gaekwads in Gujarat, one of the richest princely states and, under Sayajirao III, a pioneer of free schooling and public libraries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pilaji-rao-gaekwad">Pilaji Rao Gaekwad</a>, a commander serving the Dabhade family and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> state, seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/songadh">Songadh</a> in 1721 and used it as a base for raids into Mughal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damaji-rao-gaekwad">Damaji Rao II</a> took Baroda (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vadodara">Vadodara</a>) and much of the province, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaekwads">Gaekwads</a> became one of the great Maratha houses alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peshwa">Peshwas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holkars">Holkars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scindias">Scindias</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>After disputed successions, Baroda entered a subsidiary alliance with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> in 1802–05. In 1875 the British deposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malhar-rao-gaekwad">Malhar Rao</a> for an alleged attempt to poison the Resident. His adopted successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayajirao-gaekwad-iii">Sayajirao Gaekwad III</a> (1875–1939) introduced compulsory primary education, a state library network, courts and railways, banned child marriage and funded scholars including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/b-r-ambedkar">B. R. Ambedkar</a>. The state ranked among the few with a 21-gun salute.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pratapsinh-gaekwad">Pratapsinh Gaekwad</a> acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1947, and on 1 May 1949 Baroda was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bombay-state">Bombay State</a>; since 1960 it has been part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi-vilas-palace">Lakshmi Vilas Palace</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharaja-sayajirao-university-of-baroda">Maharaja Sayajirao University</a> are its best-known legacies.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/baroda-state.jpg" alt="Baroda State" width="320" height="206"><figcaption>Image: Robert Alfers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baroda_flag.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1721 CE – 1949 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vadodara</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pilaji Rao Gaekwad, Damaji Rao Gaekwad, Sayajirao Gaekwad III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gujarati, Marathi</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gaekwad State, Baroda, Vadodara State</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1835&amp;focus=baroda-state" aria-label="Baroda State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6640 1354 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Baroda State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6960 1610L6991 1757M6960 1610L7364 1601M6960 1610L7364 1602M6960 1610L7018 1386"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6991 1757h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M7018 1386h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6960 1610h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6960 1610h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Vadodara. <a href="/atlas?year=1835&amp;focus=baroda-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
